Z||000000||BlackDragon||11-04-2001||12:13 AM||||ok here is the deal. i have been in a rebuild on my 84 for a while. im gonna finish it in the next 2 weeks. the question is as follows. i want to take the whole exhaust and scrap it. then start from almost scratch. put the exhaust manifold back on. then run a custom exhaust strait out the passenger side in front of the tire, under the door. no cat, no muffler. and im gonna exhaust wrap the whole thing. i know its not exactly legal. but other than that why wouldn't it work? would it be a good flowing exhaust? gimme input. all opinions welcome<P>------------------<BR>who is General failure? and why is he reading my hard disk?¿?¿?¿<P>big cars may be fast, but i can corner.....<P>BlackDragonsEmail@yahoo.com||||reg||5
Z||000001||Nashco||11-04-2001||12:33 AM||||Don't use exhaust wrap if you plan on either using this car for more than a month or two.  The exhaust wrap eats away at the pipe, and is also hard to get to stay tightly wrapped for a long time.  It seems after use it likes to unravel at the ends, unless you use something to keep it held tight like wire.  The exhaust wrap is used on race cars where they don't have a ton of use, and can service the car all the time or replace parts as needed.  Otherwise, whatever you want to do with your exhaust is up to you, just make sure nothing's going to hit it and you have good isolation points.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||BlackDragon||11-04-2001||12:44 AM||||the exhaust wrap will be held in place at several points by high heat clamps designed for this purpose, i dont think it will come loose. thanks for the input.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>how quick does it eat away at the exhaust pipe?<P>------------------<BR>who is General failure? and why is he reading my hard disk?¿?¿?¿<P>big cars may be fast, but i can corner.....<P>BlackDragonsEmail@yahoo.com||||reg||1
Z||000003||Nashco||11-04-2001||01:01 AM||||Like I said, after a couple months your pipe will be start showing deterioration.  A lot of that depends on how you drive it, how well it's wrapped, how hot it gets, what elements it's exposed to, etc.  Is the wrap necessary?  There are a lot better ways to shield heat from the car if that's what you want to do, and I highly doubt you're running an application where keeping the heat in the pipes is important.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000004||Smoooooth GT||11-04-2001||01:04 AM||||Great Idea!!  I have been thinking about doing that same sort of thing on the Smoooth GT!!!  Either that or come straight out of the rear between my Euro's... Good luck! Steve<P>------------------<BR>UNDER CONSTRUCTION <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Yellow_sig.gif">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/smooth_sig_blue.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||watts||11-04-2001||01:19 AM||||Biggest problem you'll run into running a straight pipe is a lack of backpressure.<P>The Tech 4's really like to have about 1-3psi of backpressur for the O2 sensor to do it's job, and to get proper scavenging.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fierowrecker||11-04-2001||02:52 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by watts:<BR><B>Biggest problem you'll run into running a straight pipe is a lack of backpressure.<P>The Tech 4's really like to have about 1-3psi of backpressur for the O2 sensor to do it's job, and to get proper scavenging.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>AHHH, backpressure prevents scavenging...<BR>That is why headers and free flowing muffs are used...<BR>You want the exhaust flow to create a vacumme at the port...<BR>crash...<BR>||||reg||1
