Z||000010||Cooter||11-04-2001||10:53 AM||||Why wouldn't it work? Just for today, go out and pull the converter off and leave the exhaust disconnected. Drive the car around and see what you think. The Duke is not a powerhouse engine and an open exhaust will not do much for it, other than make everything in the car viberate very badly because of the noise harmonics. A loud car does not take long to get old.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Master_Sushi||11-04-2001||11:31 AM||||You might want to put a muffler on. Its not going to sounds that great completely open. It will just sound like an old beater that's muffler rusted off. At least put on a free flowing or sport muffler on or something. But good luck on whatever you do, fieros are about customizing and making them how YOU want, not other people.<P>On a side note, I don't know what's up with my 84SE. But its Really loud. It sounds pretty manly now, but somethings not right with it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">. It probably has an exhaust leak somewhere ...lol You will all get to hear it when I post some videos of it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000012||Formula88||11-04-2001||01:18 PM||||<B><FONT SIZE="3"><I>WARNING!</I></B></FONT><P>Ok, what most people forget is, if you run a straight pipe exhaust, especially a short one, you risk warping the valves in your engine.  What happens is, when you shut down the engine, as it cools, cool outside air will be drawn into the exhaust pipe and can cause thermal shock at the exhaut valve - damaging either the valve or valve seat.  If you have a muffler, or even full car length straight pipes, that gives a bit of a buffer to slow down the cool air so the valves can cool more slowly.  If you notice on most race cars with open pipes, they cap the pipes after shutdown.  That's not to keep out rain, but to keep the cool air from damaging the engine.<P>I've experienced this on an old Mercury I had in High School.  It had an exhaust leak, and since I was broke I didn't get it fixed.  Well, it warped 2 exhaust valves nearest the leak.  <P>Also, the total lack of backpressure will cause you to loose some low end.  On a race car, this isn't an issue, but on a street car it may be.  The Iron Duke isn't going to rev high enough to reap the benefits of an open exhaust.  At the very least, I'd run a cherry bomb or glass pack to offer some buffer and a bit of backpressure.<P>GOOD LUCK!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||4
